WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.234 --> 00:00:02.169
- Gary and I have known each
other, we have this reference

00:00:02.169 --> 00:00:03.804
that we've known
each other in passing.

00:00:03.804 --> 00:00:05.205
Even though Gary
lives in New York,

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:06.974
the only place that
we've actually seen each other

00:00:06.974 --> 00:00:10.344
or gotten together is
Austin for South by Southwest,

00:00:10.344 --> 00:00:12.246
San Diego, we've
shared the same stage,

00:00:12.246 --> 00:00:13.614
Mexico City.
- That's right.

00:00:13.614 --> 00:00:15.549
- And we'll be in Australia
on the same stage--

00:00:15.549 --> 00:00:17.651
- Am I going to Australia 
or is that the hologram?

00:00:17.651 --> 00:00:18.585
- You beter go to Australia.

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:20.487
- I think it's the hologram.
- Your name's on the flyer.

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.756
- I got this
new hologram racket.

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:23.724
Cool.

00:00:23.724 --> 00:00:26.627
- Alright, so I
asked him to come down.

00:00:26.627 --> 00:00:28.662
Well, my people asked his crew.

00:00:28.662 --> 00:00:30.831
Without blinking
an eye, he came here

00:00:30.831 --> 00:00:33.333
and, trust me, we're
all industry individuals.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:35.869
It wasn't about what's in it
for me, what I'm gonna get.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.337
Very, very solid guy.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.872
So, I respect the
fact that he's here,

00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:40.407
and he didn't
want anything from me.

00:00:40.407 --> 00:00:41.708
So, thank you for
being here, thank you

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:43.010
for being with my family.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.444
- You're welcome. 
(audience applause)

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:46.079
Thanks for having me.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.448
- I wanna get
into a couple things,

00:00:48.448 --> 00:00:49.950
and then we're
gonna open up the Q&amp;amp;A,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.451
and what I wanna
know, first of all,

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:54.288
this is my
Rise and Grind Series.

00:00:54.288 --> 00:00:56.223
And, I don't
wanna talk about low,

00:00:56.223 --> 00:00:58.592
not low, but things that
startups need to know,

00:00:58.592 --> 00:01:00.160
because the whole
concept of Blueprint and Co.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:01.995
is you already have
the blueprint of business.

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:03.330
You already know
the fundamentals.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.197
You need now to move forward.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.167
And, my last book,
which was "Rise and Grind"

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:11.138
was about-- no, my last book,
which was "The Power of Broke"

00:01:11.138 --> 00:01:13.006
was use the assets
that are in front of you,

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:15.242
and I know that Gary
believes in the same thing.

00:01:15.242 --> 00:01:17.444
But, today, I want to
talk about the methods

00:01:17.444 --> 00:01:21.214
that successful people have
that they do every single day.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:22.683
When you were
growing up, did you have

00:01:22.683 --> 00:01:25.152
a rise and grind mentality?

00:01:27.788 --> 00:01:29.389
- When I was
growing up, there was a lot

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:30.824
of interesting
things going on with me,

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:35.462
which is, I was an immigrant,
and education was the way out

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:36.663
for immigrants.

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:39.499
Probably, still today, 
but, extremely,

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:41.969
entrepreneurship is "new cool".

00:01:41.969 --> 00:01:44.805
It's an American thing, but
if you were an entrepreneur

00:01:44.805 --> 00:01:47.207
in the '80s and '90s,
if you said that word,

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:49.743
that means you were
a loser with an idea.

00:01:49.743 --> 00:01:51.244
(audience laughter)

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:54.414
And, so, I was in
a very weird place.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.917
My whole world,
outside of my parents,

00:01:56.917 --> 00:01:58.051
were telling me
that I was a loser.

00:01:58.051 --> 00:01:59.820
My teachers were
telling me I was a loser,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:01.621
my friends' parents were
telling me I was a loser,

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.290
'cause I was
getting D's and F's.

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.960
And the only way you were
scored as a child, at all,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.862
there was no,
you know, I never understood

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:12.633
how in eighth grade
I'm making $3,000 a weekend,

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:14.835
and I'm a loser.

00:02:14.835 --> 00:02:16.470
Because I have D's in science.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.306
So, I think, when
people ask me like struggles

00:02:19.306 --> 00:02:21.541
or things of that
nature, my narrative,

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:24.845
for most of my life,
probably from eight years old

00:02:24.845 --> 00:02:27.714
to sixteen, seventeen, eighteen,

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:31.385
was, I was positioned as
I would not be successful,

00:02:31.385 --> 00:02:33.720
because I wasn't
gonna go to Harvard.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.656
- [Daymond] Yeah,
I gotta look back,

00:02:35.656 --> 00:02:37.324
and I never saw anybody,

00:02:37.324 --> 00:02:39.259
the only entrepreneur
I saw that looked like me on TV

00:02:39.259 --> 00:02:41.461
was Fred G. Sanford,
the junk man--

00:02:41.461 --> 00:02:44.664
(Daymond drowned
out by Gary's laughter)

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:48.235
- Yeah, it wasn't
even a conversation.

00:02:48.235 --> 00:02:50.704
I wish, you know, first of all,

00:02:50.704 --> 00:02:52.339
I don't truly wish,
I envy nothing.

00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:54.508
But, it would've
been really cool

00:02:54.508 --> 00:02:57.377
to be growing up
me during this era.

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.780
Right, it would've 
been really fun.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.848
First of all, my
parents and I always joke

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:05.118
that I would never have
graduated high school, ever.

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:06.820
If eBay came out a decade early,

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:08.288
I would've sold
my entire family,

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:09.289
let alone everything
in the house.

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:10.090
(audience laughter)

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:14.261
So, yeah, look, I think
everybody deals with stuff.

00:03:17.764 --> 00:03:20.033
I love competition, right.

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:23.136
I love competition, so I think
that's really saved me.

00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:24.905
Even when 
I don't have adversity,

00:03:24.905 --> 00:03:26.640
I fabricate it.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.409
As a matter of fact,
I secretly, secretly think

00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:32.345
that I've held back
my financial upside,

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.881
because I love
the process so much.

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:37.117
I genuinely know that
I could be more wealthy,

00:03:37.117 --> 00:03:39.453
and sometimes think
back to the last ten years

00:03:39.453 --> 00:03:41.488
of some of the
decisions I'm making.

00:03:41.488 --> 00:03:43.390
I prefer the hard way.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:45.025
- [Daymond] Do you
like the process because--

00:03:45.025 --> 00:03:48.295
- [Gary] 'Cause it's my oxygen,
the process is my oxygen.

00:03:48.295 --> 00:03:49.529
- Do you often feel
that people cheat you

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:52.632
if you go, and you give, and
you hire them for the process

00:03:52.632 --> 00:03:53.633
and you don't know it?

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:54.734
'Cause I always
feel like someone

00:03:54.734 --> 00:03:56.303
is gonna hold me
hostage in my business

00:03:56.303 --> 00:03:58.972
if I don't know that process.

00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:01.808
- Yeah, I don't even
think about other people.

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:03.009
(audience laughter)

00:04:03.009 --> 00:04:04.377
I'm being dead serious.

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:06.580
Like, it's unbelievable how much

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.683
zero outside influence hits me.

00:04:09.683 --> 00:04:12.152
And, so, Instagram's really
interesting to me right now,

00:04:12.152 --> 00:04:13.553
because it's an
important platform,

00:04:13.553 --> 00:04:15.288
I'm spending a
lot of time on it,

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:17.824
but it's probably the
most motivational version

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:18.825
of myself, right?

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:20.093
I only have a minute for video,

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:21.695
and I can only do
quotes, and pictures,

00:04:21.695 --> 00:04:24.698
and so I'm fascinated by
that's where my growth is

00:04:24.698 --> 00:04:27.634
right now, yet it's
such a single sliver

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:28.869
of who I actually am.

00:04:28.869 --> 00:04:31.905
It's just one piece
of who I actually am.

00:04:31.905 --> 00:04:33.974
But, the reason
I bring that up is,

00:04:33.974 --> 00:04:36.510
it's hard to explain,
that's why this is fun.

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:37.944
We can sit here
and really get deep

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:39.846
into this stuff, in detail.

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:42.115
I don't think
about outside forces.

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:43.884
So, when I say things
in a one minute video

00:04:43.884 --> 00:04:45.952
of who gives a shit
who the President is.

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.054
One way, left,
right, I don't care.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:50.624
That's one version of
me talking about this.

00:04:50.624 --> 00:04:52.826
I'm not worried
about anybody else.

00:04:52.826 --> 00:04:56.296
- [Daymond] So, what was it
during your coming up,

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:59.533
your rise and grind,
what did you do methodically

00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:00.333
every single day?

00:05:00.333 --> 00:05:03.003
Tell me what you
did when you were 15

00:05:03.003 --> 00:05:05.338
in your first 90 minutes
when you got up,

00:05:05.338 --> 00:05:07.641
and your last 90 minutes,
to what you did today.

00:05:07.641 --> 00:05:09.042
Has it changed?

00:05:09.042 --> 00:05:11.211
Because you obviously
got educated somewhere,

00:05:11.211 --> 00:05:14.748
so you cared about
what somebody was sharing,

00:05:14.748 --> 00:05:16.850
'cause you could
absorb it one way or another.

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:18.819
- So, the one
consistent in my life,

00:05:18.819 --> 00:05:20.720
I used to have nightmares.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.289
This is actually
something I've never said.

00:05:22.289 --> 00:05:24.257
I used to have nightmares,

00:05:24.257 --> 00:05:26.359
and it was a very
reoccurring nightmare for me.

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:29.696
It was that my family and
I were going back to Belarus

00:05:29.696 --> 00:05:32.666
on a plane, and
the plane would crash.

00:05:32.666 --> 00:05:36.136
And, every time,
different people from my family,

00:05:36.136 --> 00:05:39.139
usually one to three
people, would survive.

00:05:39.139 --> 00:05:42.943
It was very dark, and
probably, in modern parenting,

00:05:42.943 --> 00:05:44.778
my parents would be
forced to send me somewhere

00:05:44.778 --> 00:05:46.446
to check out what
was wrong with me.

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:47.581
But it was consistent.

00:05:47.581 --> 00:05:50.317
I will tell you,
I had that dream 50 times.

00:05:50.317 --> 00:05:53.920
50 times between
second and seventh grade,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.557
I dreamt that my
family had a flight crash,

00:05:57.557 --> 00:05:59.359
and one or two of
them would survive,

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:03.129
and we'd walk through
snow, and it was very weird.

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:04.197
The reason I
tell you that story,

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:06.099
and the reason,
you asked the question

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:07.634
in an interesting way,
15 and now.

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:11.738
The only consistent in
my life has been perspective

00:06:11.738 --> 00:06:13.340
and gratitude.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.442
It's the only
consistent in my life.

00:06:15.442 --> 00:06:17.510
Why I'm saying
"perspective and gratitude"?

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:20.013
Every morning I wake up,
and I make pretend

00:06:20.013 --> 00:06:22.515
that my mother or
children or wife die.

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:23.516
Every day.

00:06:23.516 --> 00:06:24.784
Every day, I did it today.

00:06:24.784 --> 00:06:27.988
I quickly flash in my
mind what it would feel like,

00:06:27.988 --> 00:06:29.990
'cause I'm very
in tune with myself,

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.426
if my son Xander,
who's four years old,

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:36.763
got hit by a car
crossing the street.

00:06:36.763 --> 00:06:40.000
I promise you, if you're
in touch with your feelings,

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.635
and you can trick
yourself to feel it,

00:06:41.635 --> 00:06:42.836
even right now
I felt it a little bit

00:06:42.836 --> 00:06:47.507
and it really fucking hurts,
nothing else matters.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:50.010
I'm completely
driven by gratitude.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.779
I keep things in
very simple perspective.

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:55.181
I went up with
a very lovely lady

00:06:55.181 --> 00:06:56.016
in the elevator,
she goes,

00:06:56.016 --> 00:06:57.017
"Do you know how many
people you've inspired?"

00:06:57.017 --> 00:06:58.952
I go, "Sure do."

00:06:58.952 --> 00:07:01.288
I'm thrilled and
very in tune with my ego.

00:07:01.288 --> 00:07:03.957
I'm also very thrilled
and in tune with my humility.

00:07:03.957 --> 00:07:06.026
I think I'm the best,

00:07:06.026 --> 00:07:07.694
and I think that if 
I disappear tomorrow,

00:07:07.694 --> 00:07:08.795
nobody really gives a shit.

00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:12.299
And, so, that yin and
ying is very important to me.

00:07:12.299 --> 00:07:13.833
And, so, what I do consistently,

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:16.236
and have for thirty years now,

00:07:16.236 --> 00:07:18.672
is I keep things in perspective.

00:07:18.672 --> 00:07:20.540
This feels real nice.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.609
Admiration, fans, selfies.

00:07:22.609 --> 00:07:25.045
But, I don't know.

00:07:25.045 --> 00:07:27.347
I'm just, 
I've got it very calc,

00:07:27.347 --> 00:07:29.115
I understand what it is.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:31.818
- [Daymond]
Everybody in this room,

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:34.587
they probably
were like you and I

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:38.224
when we were focused on our
career, and getting there,

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:39.592
you know, Mark Cuban
was funny when he said,

00:07:39.592 --> 00:07:40.860
"A girlfriend said it
was me or the business,"

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.429
and he said,
"What's your name, again?"

00:07:42.429 --> 00:07:43.963
(audience laughter)

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:46.366
- So, you know, we all
got to that point, right?

00:07:46.366 --> 00:07:47.233
In our lives.

00:07:47.233 --> 00:07:50.704
At what point did you get
to the point where you said,

00:07:50.704 --> 00:07:53.973
"OK, now I have
to flip the 80-20,

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:57.243
and now I have to go and
start being with my family

00:07:57.243 --> 00:08:00.347
and I have to make track with
that timing and I have to,

00:08:00.347 --> 00:08:02.916
because you probably had
a healthy paranoia like me,

00:08:02.916 --> 00:08:05.618
like, "Listen, I've
done so much to get here.

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:07.387
"I have to stay,"

00:08:07.387 --> 00:08:09.489
and before you know it your
life can go like that, right?

00:08:09.489 --> 00:08:10.323
Blam!

00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.791
And now I know
it's extremely important

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:12.859
to go home with your family,

00:08:12.859 --> 00:08:14.294
I have a new 11 month old,
and I know you,

00:08:14.294 --> 00:08:16.429
you have a young baby at home.

00:08:16.429 --> 00:08:17.697
At what point in
your life did you say,

00:08:17.697 --> 00:08:19.366
"Wait a minute, let me
find a healthy balance,"

00:08:19.366 --> 00:08:21.167
or did it start
from the beginning?

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:24.003
- I haven't.
(audience laughter)

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:25.038
- Yeah.

00:08:25.038 --> 00:08:26.873
- I'm gonna tell you
guys the truth, I haven't,

00:08:26.873 --> 00:08:29.008
meaning I run them in parallel.

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:31.411
I'm not crippled by the
current political correctness

00:08:31.411 --> 00:08:33.279
of parenting.

00:08:33.279 --> 00:08:34.748
You're looking at a
kid who didn't see his dad

00:08:34.748 --> 00:08:35.914
until he was 14 years old,

00:08:35.914 --> 00:08:37.317
and we have the greatest
relationship of all time

00:08:37.317 --> 00:08:39.419
and I love him, and he
slept home every single night,

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:40.919
but I never saw him once.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.256
That doesn't mean
my workaholic nature

00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:44.424
will work for me and my kids.

00:08:44.424 --> 00:08:46.426
I don't take that for granted.

00:08:46.426 --> 00:08:49.462
I think that you
need to know how to breathe.

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:50.964
I know what makes me breathe.

00:08:50.964 --> 00:08:52.699
My family makes me breathe.

00:08:52.699 --> 00:08:55.235
But I've gotta
do what I wanna do,

00:08:55.235 --> 00:08:56.302
like I have to.

00:08:56.302 --> 00:08:57.670
I'll just break.

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:01.174
And so, I think
of it as in extremes.

00:09:01.174 --> 00:09:03.276
The reason I answered,
"I don't," is I don't think

00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:08.014
you find balance in any way
other than your own balance,

00:09:08.014 --> 00:09:10.884
and I think that over
communication with your partner

00:09:10.884 --> 00:09:13.553
and the variables in
that relationship are the key.

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:16.156
Right now, I have that
conversation with my wife.

00:09:16.156 --> 00:09:19.159
In coming years, I'll have those
conversations with my kids.

00:09:19.159 --> 00:09:23.196
But for me, I'm very
concerned about people

00:09:23.196 --> 00:09:25.532
posturing to work-life
balance, when I know

00:09:25.532 --> 00:09:28.501
that work-life balance
is extremely individual.

00:09:28.501 --> 00:09:30.804
Everybody in here has
different work-life balance.

00:09:30.804 --> 00:09:32.338
You also have different
work-life balance

00:09:32.338 --> 00:09:33.873
at different times in your life.

00:09:33.873 --> 00:09:36.743
I didn't have as
much work-life balance

00:09:36.743 --> 00:09:38.711
as some other
people in the last 5 years,

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:40.947
but it now also
allows me to afford

00:09:40.947 --> 00:09:43.116
to have work-life 
balance with my children

00:09:43.116 --> 00:09:44.918
and my family in a
very different way.

00:09:44.918 --> 00:09:46.619
As my daughter
Misha's now seven,

00:09:46.619 --> 00:09:49.089
and she can go on
business trips with me.

00:09:49.089 --> 00:09:50.957
The only conversation
I had to have was with my wife

00:09:50.957 --> 00:09:54.427
saying, "School's bullshit.
Be okay with me taking Misha

00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:55.762
"out of school for two days."

00:09:55.762 --> 00:09:58.498
She might miss a science test,
